ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football
==================================================
The Alabama Crimson Tide football program represents the  [LINK: (University of Alabama) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Alabama]  (variously Alabama, UA, or Bama) in  [LINK: (American football) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_football] . It is part of the wider  [LINK: (Crimson Tide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide]  athletics program and competes in the  [LINK: (Southeastern Conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeastern_Conference]  (SEC), a conference of the  [LINK: (NCAA's Football Bowl Subdivision) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_Division_I_Football_Bowl_Subdivision]  (FBS).

The Crimson Tide are among the most storied and decorated football programs in  [LINK: (NCAA history) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_history] . It claims 18  [LINK: (national championships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/College_football_national_championships_in_NCAA_Division_I_FBS] , [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Bama-NCs-text-6]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]  including 13 wire-service ( [LINK: (AP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AP_National_Championship_Trophy]  or  [LINK: (Coaches') -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AFCA_National_Championship_Trophy] ) national titles in the poll-era and five titles from before the poll-era. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]  [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_current_champs-9]  [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-10]  From 1958 to 1982, Hall of Fame coach  [LINK: (Paul "Bear" Bryant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Bryant]  led the program to six national titles. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  Head coach  [LINK: (Nick Saban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Saban]  oversaw another golden era between 2007 and 2023, winning six further national titles. It was not until 2009, however, that running back  [LINK: (Mark Ingram II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Ingram_II]  became the first Alabama player to receive a  [LINK: (Heisman Trophy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heisman_Trophy] . In 2015,  [LINK: (Derrick Henry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derrick_Henry]  became the university's second Heisman winner. [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Ingram-Heisman-11]  The program achieved two further Heisman Trophies in 2020 and 2021; these were awarded to  [LINK: (DeVonta Smith) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeVonta_Smith]  and  [LINK: (Bryce Young) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Young] , respectively.

Alabama has 985 official victories [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-notea-12]  [LINK: ([b]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-noteb-13]  in NCAA Division I (an additional 21 victories were vacated, and eight victories and one tie were forfeited). Alabama has won 34 conference championships (4  [LINK: (Southern Conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Conference]  and 30 SEC championships), and has made an NCAA-record 79 postseason bowl appearances. The program has 43 seasons with ten wins or more (plus one vacated) [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-14]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-15]  and has 46 [LINK: ([b]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#Notes]  bowl victories, both NCAA records. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011_Records-16] Alabama holds the NCAA record for most consecutive ten win seasons at sixteen from 2008 to 2023. The Crimson Tide lead the SEC West Division with 18 division titles and 16 appearances in the  [LINK: (SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEC_Championship_Game] . The  [LINK: (Associated Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press]  (AP) ranks Alabama fourth in all-time final AP Poll appearances, with 62 through the 2024 season. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-17]

Alabama plays home games on  [LINK: (Saban Field at Bryant–Denny Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryant%E2%80%93Denny_Stadium] , located on UA's campus in  [LINK: (Tuscaloosa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuscaloosa,_Alabama] . Its capacity of 100,077 makes it the  [LINK: (tenth largest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_stadiums_by_capacity]  non-racing stadium in the world and the  [LINK: (eighth largest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_North_American_stadiums_by_capacity]  stadium in the United States. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-BDS-1]  The team's rallying cry is " [LINK: (Roll Tide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roll_Tide] !". Its official fight song is " [LINK: (Yea Alabama) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Alabama_traditions] ", although " [LINK: (Dixieland Delight) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixieland_Delight] " is widely sung as an unofficial anthem. The Crimson Tide's fiercest rivalry is with the Alabama-based  [LINK: (Auburn Tigers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auburn_Tigers_football] , against whom it contests the  [LINK: (Iron Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Bowl] .


# HISTORY #

Alabama has had 28 head coaches since organized football began in 1892. Adopting the nickname "Crimson Tide" after the 1907 season, 12 coaches have led the Crimson Tide in postseason bowl games:  [LINK: (Wallace Wade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallace_Wade] ,  [LINK: (Frank Thomas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Thomas_(American_football)] ,  [LINK: (Harold D. "Red" Drew) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harold_Drew] ,  [LINK: (Bear Bryant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Bryant] ,  [LINK: (Ray Perkins) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ray_Perkins_(wide_receiver)] ,  [LINK: (Bill Curry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Curry] ,  [LINK: (Gene Stallings) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gene_Stallings] ,  [LINK: (Mike DuBose) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_DuBose] ,  [LINK: (Dennis Franchione) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennis_Franchione] ,  [LINK: (Mike Shula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Shula] ,  [LINK: (Joe Kines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Kines] , and  [LINK: (Nick Saban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Saban] . [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Bama-NCs-text-6]  Eight of those coaches also won  [LINK: (conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athletic_conference]  championships: Wade, Thomas, Drew, Bryant, Curry, Stallings, DuBose, and Saban. During their tenures, Wade, Thomas, Bryant, Stallings, and Saban all won  [LINK: (national championships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_Division_I_FBS_National_Football_Championship]  with the Crimson Tide. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Bama-NCs-text-6]

Of the 27 different head coaches who have led the Crimson Tide, Wade, [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-18]  Thomas, [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-19]  Bryant, [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-20]  and Stallings have been inducted into the  [LINK: (College Football Hall of Fame) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/College_Football_Hall_of_Fame] . The current head coach is  [LINK: (Kalen DeBoer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalen_DeBoer] , who took over the position in 2024 following the retirement of long-time head coach Nick Saban following the 2023 season. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Saban-hired-21]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-22]  [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-ESPN49Hours-23]

National championships in  [LINK: (NCAA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA]   [LINK: (FBS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_Division_I_Football_Bowl_Subdivision]  college football are debated as the NCAA does not officially award the championship. Despite not naming an official  [LINK: (National Champion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_Division_I_FBS_National_Football_Championship] , the NCAA provides lists of championships awarded by "major selectors." [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA-history-24]  According to the official NCAA 2009 Division I Football Records Book, "During the last 138 years, there have been more than 30 selectors of national champions using polls, historical research and mathematical rating systems. Beginning in 1936, the Associated Press began the best-known and most widely circulated poll of sportswriters and broadcasters. Before 1936, national champions were determined by historical research and retroactive ratings and polls. [...] The criteria for being included in this historical list of poll selectors is that the poll be national in scope, either through distribution in newspaper, television, radio and/or computer online." [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2009_Division_I_Football_Records_Book-25]

Since World War II, Alabama claims only national championships awarded by the final  [LINK: (AP Poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AP_Poll]  or the final  [LINK: (Coaches' Poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coaches%27_Poll] .[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ] This policy is consistent with other FBS football programs with numerous national title claims,[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ] including Notre Dame,  [LINK: (USC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USC_Trojans_football] , and  [LINK: (Oklahoma) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oklahoma_Sooners_football] , except that in the pre-1936 era, unlike Alabama, there are major selectors' titles that these schools do not claim. All national championships claimed by the University of Alabama were published in nationally syndicated newspapers and magazines, and each of the national championship selectors, and are cited in the Official 2010 NCAA FBS Record Book. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-26]  In addition to the championships claimed by the university, the NCAA has listed Alabama as receiving a championship for the  [LINK: (1945) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1945_college_football_season] ,  [LINK: (1966) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1966_NCAA_University_Division_football_season] ,  [LINK: (1975) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1975_NCAA_Division_I_football_season] , and  [LINK: (1977 college football seasons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1977_NCAA_Division_I_football_season] . [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA-history-24]

In Alabama's 1982 media guide, the last for Coach Bryant, 1934 is listed as the only national championship before Coach Bryant in a footnote about the school's SEC history. In the 1980s, Alabama's Sports Information Director Wayne Atcheson started recognizing five pre-Bryant national championship teams (1925, 1926, 1930, 1934, 1941) by adding them to the university's Football Media Guide. According to Atcheson, he made the effort in the context of disputed titles being claimed by other schools, and "to make Alabama football look the best it could look" to compete with the other claimants. Atcheson maintains that the titles are the school's rightful claims. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Solomon-2010-27]  Four of the five championships claimed in the Media Guide come before the AP poll was introduced in 1936. Many schools claim national championships from pre-1936 because there was no contemporary or nationally recognized authoritative source before that year.

The  [LINK: (University of Alabama) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Alabama]  2009 Official Football Media Guide says Alabama had 12 national championships prior to winning the  [LINK: (2010 BCS National Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_BCS_National_Championship_Game] . [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-28]  The 2009, 2011, 2012, 2015, 2017, and 2020 titles bring the total number of national championships claimed by Alabama to 18. Thirteen of Alabama's national championships were awarded by the wire-services ( [LINK: (AP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AP_Poll] ,  [LINK: (Coaches' Poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coaches%27_Poll] ) or by winning the  [LINK: (BCS National Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BCS_National_Championship_Game] . [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA-history-24]

In January 2013, CNN suggested that Alabama might be college football's new dynasty, [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Is_Alabama_college_football's_new_dynasty?-29]  and in May 2013, Athlon Sports ranked Alabama's ongoing dynasty as the fourth-best since 1934, behind  [LINK: (Oklahoma) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oklahoma_Sooners_football]  (1948–58),  [LINK: (Miami) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami_Hurricanes_football]  (1986–92), and  [LINK: (Nebraska) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nebraska_Cornhuskers_football]  (1993–97). [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Athlon_Sports_ranks_the_best_dynasties_in_college_football_since_the_AP_Poll_debuted-30]

1925 – The  [LINK: (1925 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1925_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Wallace Wade, completed the regular season 9–0–0, winning the  [LINK: (Southern Conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Conference]  championship. Alabama was then invited to play  [LINK: (Washington) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Huskies_football]  in the January 1,  [LINK: (1926 Rose Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1926_Rose_Bowl] . Coach Wade's team initially fell behind the undefeated Huskies, but rallied in the second half to defeat Washington 20–19. The outstanding player of the game was  [LINK: (Johnny Mack Brown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnny_Mack_Brown] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  This game is viewed by many football historians as the single most important event for Southern football, and is hailed "the football game that changed the South." Alabama was the first Southern football team to be invited to play in the Rose Bowl and proved Southern teams could compete with those from the East, the Midwest and the West coast. The victory for Coach Wallace Wade established Alabama as a football powerhouse. The 1925 Alabama football team finished the season with a 10–0–0 record and was retrospectively selected national champion by  [LINK: (William Boand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boand_System] ,  [LINK: (Richard Billingsley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsley_Report] , and the one-man  [LINK: (Helms Athletic Foundation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helms_Athletic_Foundation] . [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  The 2009 NCAA Record Book cites the Michigan Wolverines, Dartmouth Indians, and Alabama Crimson Tide as national champions in 1925. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]

1926 – The  [LINK: (1926 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1926_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Wallace Wade, completed the regular season 9–0–0, winning the Southern Conference championship. Alabama was then invited to play  [LINK: (Stanford) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Cardinal_football]  in the January 1, 1927, Rose Bowl. Coach Wade's team tied the Indians 7–7 to finish the season 9–0–1. The outstanding player of the game was Fred Pickhard. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1926 Alabama football team was retrospectively selected national champion by Billingsley and the  [LINK: (Helms Athletic Foundation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helms_Athletic_Foundation] . [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  The 2009 NCAA Record Book cites the Lafayette Leopards, Michigan Wolverines, Naval Academy Midshipmen,  [LINK: (Stanford Cardinal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Cardinal_football] , and Alabama Crimson Tide as national champions in 1926. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]

1930 – The  [LINK: (1930 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1930_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Wallace Wade, completed the regular season 9–0–0, winning the Southern Conference championship. Alabama was then invited to play  [LINK: (Washington State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_State_Cougars_football]  in the January 1, 1931, Rose Bowl. Coach Wade's team defeated the Cougars 24–0 to finish the season 10–0–0. The outstanding player of the game was John Campbell. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1930 Alabama and Notre Dame teams were selected as "National Champion Foot Ball Teams" by  [LINK: (Parke H. Davis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parke_H._Davis]  in 1934. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Spalding1934-35]

1934 – The  [LINK: (1934 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1934_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by  [LINK: (Frank Thomas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Thomas_(football_coach)] , completed the regular season 9–0–0, winning the  [LINK: (Southeastern Conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeastern_Conference]  championship. Alabama was then invited to play  [LINK: (Stanford) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Cardinal_football]  in the January 1, 1935, Rose Bowl. Coach Thomas' team defeated the Indians 29–13 to finish the season 10–0–0. The outstanding player of the game was  [LINK: (Millard "Dixie" Howell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixie_Howell] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1934 Alabama football team was contemporaneously selected national champion under mathematical systems by  [LINK: (Dick Dunkel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunkel_System] ,  [LINK: (Deke Houlgate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houlgate_System] , and the Williamson system. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  The 2009 NCAA Record Book cites the  [LINK: (Minnesota Golden Gophers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Golden_Gophers_football]  and Alabama Crimson Tide as national champions in 1934. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]  The University of Alabama honored Ben McLeod Jr., the 95-year–old former backup End of the 1934 team at the September 6, 2008, Alabama–Tulane game. [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-36]

1941 – The  [LINK: (1941 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1941_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Frank Thomas, completed the regular season 8–2–0. Alabama's squad finished third in the Southeastern Conference. [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-37]  After losing to  [LINK: (Mississippi State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mississippi_State_Bulldogs_football]  14-0 and  [LINK: (Vanderbilt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanderbilt_Commodores_football] , [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  7–0, Alabama finished the regular season ranked No. 20 in the  [LINK: (AP poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AP_poll] , which was published on  [LINK: (December 1, 1941) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1941_college_football_rankings] , before the bowl games, which was customary during that time.[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ] The onset of World War II changed the college football postseason. [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-January_4,_1942-38]  Alabama was one of ten teams chosen for post-season competition when they were invited to play  [LINK: (Texas A&M) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_A%26M_Aggies_football]  in the January 1, 1942,  [LINK: (Cotton Bowl Classic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton_Bowl_Classic] . Coach Thomas' team defeated the Aggies 29–21 to finish the season 9–2–0. Minnesota, the AP national champion, finished 8–0 and did not play in a bowl game per Big Ten rules. Alabama's outstanding players of the game were  [LINK: (Holt Rast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holt_Rast] ,  [LINK: (Don Whitmire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Whitmire] , and Jimmy Nelson. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The squad was selected national champion under a mathematical system created by  [LINK: (Deke Houlgate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houlgate_System] , who in later years published the nationally syndicated Football Thesaurus. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  The 2009 NCAA Record Book cites the  [LINK: (Minnesota Golden Gophers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota_Golden_Gophers_football] ,  [LINK: (Texas Longhorns) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_Longhorns_football] , and Alabama Crimson Tide as national champions in 1941. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-NCAA_Past-champs-8]

1961 – The  [LINK: (1961 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1961_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Bear Bryant, completed the regular season 10–0–0, winning the Southeastern Conference championship. Led by quarterback  [LINK: (Pat Trammell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pat_Trammell] , linebacker  [LINK: (Lee Roy Jordan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Roy_Jordan]  and two–way lineman  [LINK: (Billy Neighbors) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billy_Neighbors] , Alabama outscored their opponents 297–25. Alabama was then invited to play the No. 9–ranked  [LINK: (Arkansas Razorbacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkansas_Razorbacks_football]  in the January 1,  [LINK: (1962 Sugar Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1962_Sugar_Bowl] . Coach Bryant's team defeated the Razorbacks 10–3 to finish the season 11–0–0. The outstanding player of the game was Mike Fracchia. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1961 Alabama football team was selected national champion by the  [LINK: (AP Poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AP_Poll]  and  [LINK: (Coaches Poll) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coaches_Poll] . [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]

1964 – The  [LINK: (1964 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1964_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Bear Bryant, completed the regular season 10–0–0, winning the Southeastern Conference championship. Alabama was led by quarterback  [LINK: (Joe Namath) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Namath] . Alabama was then invited to play the  [LINK: (Texas Longhorns) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_Longhorns_football]  in the  [LINK: (Orange Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_Bowl_(game)]  on January 1, 1965. Coach Bryant's team lost to the Longhorns 21–17 to finish the season 10–1–0. The outstanding player of the game was  [LINK: (Joe Namath) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Namath] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1964 Alabama football team was selected national champion by the AP Poll and the Coaches Poll prior to bowl games. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  (The AP Poll waited until after the bowl games to select its champion for the 1965 season.)

1965 – The  [LINK: (1965 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1965_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Bear Bryant, completed the regular season 8–1–1, winning the Southeastern Conference championship. The Tide lost to  [LINK: (Georgia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Bulldogs_football]  and tied  [LINK: (Tennessee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennessee_Volunteers_football]  during the regular season. Alabama was then invited to play  [LINK: (Nebraska) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nebraska_Cornhuskers_football]  in the January 1,  [LINK: (1966 Orange Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1966_Orange_Bowl] . Coach Bryant's team defeated the Cornhuskers 39–28 to finish the season 9–1–1. The outstanding player of the game was  [LINK: (Steve Sloan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Sloan] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1965 Alabama football team was selected national champion by the AP Poll. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  The Coaches Poll selected Michigan State.

1973 – The  [LINK: (1973 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1973_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Bear Bryant, completed the regular season 11–0–0, winning the Southeastern Conference championship. Alabama was then invited to play Notre Dame in the December 31, 1973, Sugar Bowl. Coach Bryant's team lost to the Fighting Irish 24–23 to finish the season 11–1–0. The 1973 Alabama football team was selected national champion in the final regular-season Coaches Poll, which was finalized prior to the post-season bowl games. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  (The Coaches Poll began selecting their champion after the bowl games starting in 1974.) The post-bowl game AP Poll ranked Alabama fourth, and selected Notre Dame as its national champion.[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

1978 – The  [LINK: (1978 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1978_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Bear Bryant, completed the regular season 10–1–0, winning the Southeastern Conference championship. The Tide defeated No. 10–ranked Nebraska 20–3, and defeated No. 11–ranked  [LINK: (Missouri) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missouri_Tigers_football]  38–20, and lost to No. 7 ranked  [LINK: (Southern California) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USC_Trojans_football]  24–14, during the regular season. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2010_USC_Football_Media_Guide-39]  The No. 2 ranked Alabama Crimson Tide was then invited to play the No. 1–ranked  [LINK: (Penn State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penn_State_Nittany_Lions_football]  in the January 1,  [LINK: (1979 Sugar Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1979_Sugar_Bowl] . Coach Bryant's team defeated the Nittany Lions 14–7 to finish the season 11–1–0. The outstanding player of the game was linebacker  [LINK: (Barry Krauss) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barry_Krauss] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  Alabama was selected national champion by the AP Poll, [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]  and Southern California was selected national champion by the Coaches Poll. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2010_USC_Football_Media_Guide-39]

1979 – The  [LINK: (1979 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1979_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Bear Bryant, completed the regular season 11–0–0, winning the Southeastern Conference championship. The Tide defeated No. 18–ranked Tennessee 27–17, and defeated No. 14–ranked Auburn 25–18 during the regular season. Alabama was then invited to play No. 6–ranked Arkansas in the January 1,  [LINK: (1980 Sugar Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1980_Sugar_Bowl] . Coach Bryant's team defeated the Razorbacks 24–9 to finish the season 12–0–0. The outstanding player of the game was running back Major Ogilvie. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1979 Alabama football team was selected national champion by the AP Poll and the Coaches Poll. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]

1992 – The  [LINK: (1992 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1992_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Gene Stallings, completed the regular season 11–0–0. They then defeated No. 12–ranked  [LINK: (Florida) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1992_Florida_Gators_football_team]  in the  [LINK: (inaugural SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1992_SEC_Championship_Game] , defeating the Gators 28–21; the win gave Alabama its 20th SEC title and a record of 12–0–0. Alabama was then invited to play No. 1–ranked  [LINK: (Miami) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami_Hurricanes_football] , led by Heisman Trophy winner Gino Torretta, in the  [LINK: (Bowl Coalition National Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowl_Coalition] ,  [LINK: (1993 Sugar Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1993_Sugar_Bowl] . Coach Stallings' team defeated the Hurricanes 34–13 to finish the season 13–0–0. The outstanding player of the game was  [LINK: (Derrick Lassic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derrick_Lassic] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-bowl_tradition-34]  The 1992 Alabama football team was awarded the national championship by the AP Poll and the Coaches Poll. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2003_media_guide-7]

2009 – The  [LINK: (2009 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by  [LINK: (Nick Saban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Saban] , finished with a 12–0 regular season. In the twelve wins, the Crimson Tide defeated four teams that were ranked at the time, including an opening day victory over No. 7  [LINK: (Virginia Tech) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_Virginia_Tech_Hokies_football_team]  in the  [LINK: (Chick-fil-A Kickoff Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chick-fil-A_Kickoff_Game] . The team headed back to the  [LINK: (Georgia Dome) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Dome]  in December to face off against  [LINK: (No. 1 Florida) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_Florida_Gators_football_team]  in the  [LINK: (SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_SEC_Championship_Game] . The Crimson Tide defeated the Gators 32–13 in a rematch of the  [LINK: (previous year's championship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2008_SEC_Championship_Game] . [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2009-SEC-USAT-40]  Alabama then traveled to  [LINK: (Pasadena) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasadena,_California]  to face No. 2 ranked  [LINK: (Texas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_Texas_Longhorns_football_team]  in the  [LINK: (2010 BCS National Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_BCS_National_Championship_Game]  at the  [LINK: (Rose Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_Bowl_(stadium)] . Alabama's Heisman Trophy-winning running back,  [LINK: (Mark Ingram II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Ingram_II] , rushed for 116 yards and two touchdowns in a 37–21 win. [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2010-NC-USAT-41]  This was Alabama's first victory over Texas (1–7–1). Ingram was named the game's offensive MVP in Alabama's first  [LINK: (BCS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowl_Championship_Series]  victory. The 2009 Alabama football team was selected national champion by the AP and Coaches Polls. The 2009 squad became the first FBS division team to defeat six teams ranked in the AP top 25 during one season and received a record six first-team AP All-America selections. The 2009 team finished with a perfect 14–0 record, an all-time highest number of wins in a season for Alabama.

2011 – The  [LINK: (2011 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Nick Saban, completed the regular season 11–1. The only loss of the season was to  [LINK: (LSU in overtime 9–6) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_LSU_vs._Alabama_football_game] . [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011LSUESPN-42]  The team did not play in the SEC Championship Game because of that loss, but won convincingly in its final three regular-season games and earned a No. 2 ranking in the BCS poll. [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011LSURematch-43]  For their final regular season game, Alabama defeated rival  [LINK: (Auburn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_Auburn_Tigers_football_team]  42–14. [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011AUESPN-44]  Alabama, led by Heisman trophy finalist  [LINK: (Trent Richardson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trent_Richardson] , then qualified to play No. 1 ranked LSU in the  [LINK: (2012 BCS National Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_BCS_National_Championship_Game] . [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011LSURematch-43]  Coach Saban's team defeated the Tigers 21–0 and finished the season 12–1. [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011BCSESPN-45]   [LINK: (Jeremy Shelley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeremy_Shelley]  had a bowl record-tying five field goals in the game, and the game's offensive MVP was  [LINK: (A. J. McCarron) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A._J._McCarron] , and the defensive MVP was  [LINK: (Courtney Upshaw) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Courtney_Upshaw] . [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011BCSESPN-45]  With the win, Alabama became the first team to shutout its opponent in a BCS bowl game. [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011BCSESPN-45]  In addition to winning the BCS National Championship, the AP also awarded its national title to Alabama for the eighth time. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2011FinalPollESPN-46]

2012 – The  [LINK: (2012 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Nick Saban, completed the regular season 11–1. The only loss of the season was against  [LINK: (Texas A&M) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_Texas_A%26M_Aggies_football_team]  29–24. [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2012TXESPN-47]  Despite the loss, Alabama won the SEC Western division and went to the  [LINK: (2012 SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_SEC_Championship_Game] , where they defeated  [LINK: (Georgia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_Georgia_Bulldogs_football_team]  32–28 for the 23rd conference championship in school history. [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2012GAESPN-48]  Alabama earned a No. 2 ranking in the final BCS rankings for the second straight year and as a result qualified for the  [LINK: (2013 BCS National Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2013_BCS_National_Championship_Game]  against No. 1  [LINK: (Notre Dame) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_Notre_Dame_Fighting_Irish_football_team] . [LINK: ([47]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2012BCSAnnounced-49]  Alabama defeated the Fighting Irish 42–14, finished the season 13–1, and the game's offensive MVP was  [LINK: (Eddie Lacy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eddie_Lacy] , and the defensive MVP was  [LINK: (C. J. Mosley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Mosley_(linebacker)] . [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2012NDESPN-50]  Alabama became the third team in history to win three national championships in a four-year period. This was Alabama's ninth AP national championship and tenth wire-service championship. [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-2012NDESPN-50]

2015 – The  [LINK: (2015 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Nick Saban, finished the regular season 11–1. Their only loss was to  [LINK: (Ole Miss) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Ole_Miss_Rebels_football_team]  43–37. They won the SEC Western Division title with a record of 7–1, defeating rivals LSU and Tennessee en route, and then defeated  [LINK: (Florida) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Florida_Gators_football_team]  29–15 in the  [LINK: (SEC Championship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_SEC_Championship_Game] . Alabama returned to the playoffs for the second consecutive season. After falling short in the semifinals against Ohio State in 2014, Alabama defeated the  [LINK: (Michigan State Spartans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Michigan_State_Spartans_football_team]  38–0 in the  [LINK: (Cotton Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Cotton_Bowl_Classic_(December)]  to advance to the  [LINK: (Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_College_Football_Playoff_National_Championship] . Alabama beat the  [LINK: (Clemson Tigers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Clemson_Tigers_football_team]  45–40 and won the 2015 FBS national championship. Alabama's Heisman Trophy-winning running back Derrick Henry rushed for 158 yards and three touchdowns. This victory gave Coach Nick Saban his fifth national title, including four in the last seven seasons.

2017 – The  [LINK: (2017 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Nick Saban, finished the regular season 11–1. Their only loss was at  [LINK: (Auburn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Auburn_Tigers_football_team]  26–14. They won a share of the SEC Western Division title with a record of 7–1. Alabama returned to the playoffs for the fourth consecutive season. Alabama avenged the previous season's only loss in the National Championship game to  [LINK: (Clemson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Clemson_Tigers_football_team]  with a 24–6 win in the  [LINK: (Sugar Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Sugar_Bowl] . The Tide advanced to the  [LINK: (Championship game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_College_Football_Playoff_National_Championship]  for the third year in a row. Alabama defeated SEC opponent  [LINK: (Georgia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Georgia_Bulldogs_football_team]  26–23 in overtime. The victory gave Nick Saban his sixth national title, tying him with  [LINK: (Paul W. Bryant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Bryant]  for most all-time. It is also Alabama's fifth title in the last nine seasons. [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-51]

2020 – The  [LINK: (2020 Alabama Crimson Tide football team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , coached by Nick Saban, finished the regular season 10–0. The season was shortened to an all-conference schedule due to the  [LINK: (COVID-19 pandemic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic] . The Tide won the SEC Western Division title and defeated  [LINK: (Florida) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Florida_Gators_football_team]  52–46 in the  [LINK: (SEC Championship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_SEC_Championship_Game] . Alabama returned to the playoffs for the sixth time in seven seasons. They defeated  [LINK: (Notre Dame) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Notre_Dame_Fighting_Irish_football_team]  31–14 in the  [LINK: (Rose Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Rose_Bowl] . The Tide advanced to the  [LINK: (Championship game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_College_Football_Playoff_National_Championship]  where they beat  [LINK: (Ohio State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Ohio_State_Buckeyes_football_team]  52–24. Alabama's Heisman Trophy-winning wide receiver  [LINK: (DeVonta Smith) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeVonta_Smith]  had 215 yards and 3 touchdowns. The victory gave Saban his seventh national title, passing Bear Bryant for most all-time. It is also Alabama's sixth title in twelve years.

Alabama has won a total of 34 conference championships; this includes four Southern Conference and 30 SEC Championships. Alabama captured its four Southern Conference titles in 1924, 1925, 1926, and 1930. Alabama captured the first SEC title in 1933 and has won a total of 30 SEC Championships (1933, 1934, 1937, 1945, 1953, 1961, 1964, 1965, 1966, 1971, 1972, 1973, 1974, 1975, 1977, 1978, 1979, 1981, 1989†, 1992, 1999, 2009, 2012, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2018, 2020, 2021, 2023). The school has won more SEC football titles than any other school, including 11 since the conference split into separate divisions and added the  [LINK: (Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEC_Championship_Game]  in 1992. Alabama is the only school to win an SEC Championship in every decade since the conference was founded in 1933.

† Co-champions

The SEC was split into two divisions beginning in the 1992 season. Alabama competed in the SEC West. Alabama won 18 division titles and posted an 11–4 record in the  [LINK: (SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEC_Championship_Game] .

† Co-champions


# INDIVIDUAL ACCOMPLISHMENTS #

Every year, several publications release lists of their ideal "team". The athletes on these lists are referred to as  [LINK: (All-Americans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/All-America] . The NCAA recognizes five All-American lists. They are the  [LINK: (Associated Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press]  (AP),  [LINK: (American Football Coaches Association) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Football_Coaches_Association]  (AFCA),  [LINK: (Football Writers Association of America) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Football_Writers_Association_of_America]  (FWAA),  [LINK: (Sporting News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sporting_News]  (TSN), and the  [LINK: (Walter Camp Football Foundation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Camp_Football_Foundation]  (WCFF). Alabama has had 150 players honored 174 times as first team All-Americans (85 consensus) [LINK: ([50]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-DocStoc-52]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Awards_and_the_NFL-53]  in its history, including 20 players honored twice and two players ( [LINK: (Cornelius Bennett) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornelius_Bennett]  and  [LINK: (Woodrow Lowe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodrow_Lowe] ) who were honored three times as a First Team All-American. [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-54]

The most recent All-American from Alabama came after the  [LINK: (2025 season) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] , when  [LINK: (Kadyn Proctor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadyn_Proctor] , was named Consensus first-team All-American by various selectors.

In 1951, the  [LINK: (College Football Hall of Fame) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/College_Football_Hall_of_Fame]  opened in  [LINK: (South Bend, Indiana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Bend,_Indiana] . Since then, Alabama has had 23 players and 5 former coaches inducted into the Hall of Fame. [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-55]  [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-56]  Alabama had two members inducted into the inaugural 1951 class— [LINK: (Don Hutson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Hutson]  [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-57]  and  [LINK: (Frank Thomas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Thomas_(American_football)] . [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-58]

