On December 12, 2009,  [LINK: (Mark Ingram II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Ingram_II]  became Alabama's first  [LINK: (Heisman Trophy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heisman_Trophy]   [LINK: (winner) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Heisman_Trophy_winners] . [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Ingram-Heisman-11]  In the closest race ever, he edged out  [LINK: (Stanford) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_Cardinal_football]  running back  [LINK: (Toby Gerhart) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toby_Gerhart]  by 28 points. [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Ingram-Heisman-11]  Other notable finishes for an Alabama player occurred in 1993, when  [LINK: (David Palmer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Palmer_(American_football)]  finished third in the Heisman voting [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-59]  [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-60]  and when  [LINK: (A. J. McCarron) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A._J._McCarron]  finished as runner-up for the 2013 season. [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-AJHeisman-61]   [LINK: (Derrick Henry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derrick_Henry]  became Alabama's second Heisman trophy winner on December 12, 2015. [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-62]   [LINK: (Tua Tagovailoa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tua_Tagovailoa]  finished runner-up for the 2018 season as well. For the 2020 season, Alabama became the second program in college football history to have three players finish in the top five.  [LINK: (DeVonta Smith) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeVonta_Smith]  became Alabama's third Heisman winner with  [LINK: (Mac Jones) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mac_Jones]  finishing third and  [LINK: (Najee Harris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Najee_Harris]  fifth. In 2021,  [LINK: (Bryce Young) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Young]  became the fourth winner with  [LINK: (Will Anderson Jr.) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will_Anderson_Jr.]  finishing fifth.  With its fourth Heisman winner Alabama has produced the fifth most Heisman trophies of all time behind Notre Dame, Oklahoma, Ohio State and USC. [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-63]

