# RIVALRIES #

The main rivalry of the Crimson Tide is against its in-state rival, Auburn University; considered one of the top sporting rivalries in the US. The Alabama–Auburn game has come to be known as the  [LINK: (Iron Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Bowl] . [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-64]  [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-65]  The outcome of the game generally determines "bragging rights" in the state of Alabama until the following contest. The game may also have implications as to which team will represent the SEC Western Division in the  [LINK: (SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEC_Championship_Game] .

On February 22, 1893, at Lakeview Park in  [LINK: (Birmingham) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birmingham,_Alabama] , Auburn was victorious in the first ever Iron Bowl, 32–22. The series was suspended after the 1907 contest, due to violence and financial complications. [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-66]  In 1944, Auburn suggested to reopen the series, though the board of trustees at Alabama rejected. The series was resumed in 1948, with Alabama crushing the Tigers 55–0, which is still the largest margin of victory in the series. [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-67]  [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-68]  In the following contest, Auburn shocked Alabama with a 14–13 victory, which is credited with helping revive the series. [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-69]

For many years, the contest was held at  [LINK: (Legion Field) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legion_Field]  in Birmingham, before the teams began alternating between Bryant–Denny Stadium, in Tuscaloosa, and  [LINK: (Jordan–Hare Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jordan%E2%80%93Hare_Stadium] , in  [LINK: (Auburn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auburn,_Alabama] . Alabama won the most recent meeting 27–20 in Auburn and leads the series at 52–37–1. [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-70]

Despite the heated in-state rivalry with Auburn, Bear Bryant was more adamant about defeating his rivals to the north, the  [LINK: (Tennessee Volunteers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennessee_Volunteers_football] . [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-71]  The series is named the  [LINK: (Third Saturday in October) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Saturday_in_October] , the traditional calendar date on which the game was played. Despite the name, the game has been played on the third Saturday only five times between 1995 and 2007. The first game between the two sides was played in 1901 in Birmingham, ending in a 6–6 tie. From 1902 to 1913, Alabama dominated the series, losing only once, and never allowing a touchdown by the Volunteers. Beginning in 1928, the rivalry was first played on its traditional date and began to be a challenge for the Crimson Tide as  [LINK: (Robert Neyland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Neyland]  began challenging Alabama for their perennial spot on top of the conference standings. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-72]  In the 1950s, Jim Goostree, the head trainer for Alabama, began another tradition as he began handing out cigars following a victory over the Volunteers. [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-73]

Between 1971 and 1981, Alabama held an 11-game winning streak over the Volunteers and, between 1986 and 1994, a nine-game unbeaten streak. However, following Alabama's streak, Tennessee responded with a seven-game winning streak from 1995 to 2001. Alabama holds the longest winning streak at 15 from 2007 to 2021. Alabama won the most recent meeting 37–20 in Tuscaloosa and leads the series 60–40–7. [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-74]

A rivalry within the SEC Western Division occurs yearly between Alabama and the LSU Tigers. Starting in 1895, the Tigers were victorious 12–6 in the first meeting. [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-Record-book-75]  The teams did not regularly meet until the mid-1960s during Alabama's dominance of the SEC. Between 1971 and 1981, the Crimson Tide won 11 consecutive times. In the 1969 game, LSU defeated Alabama 20–15 in  [LINK: (Baton Rouge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baton_Rouge,_Louisiana] . Alabama did not lose again in Baton Rouge until 2000.

In 2007, the meeting was more heated following Alabama's hiring of head coach  [LINK: (Nick Saban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Saban] , who previously coached at LSU. With the hiring, many media outlets dubbed the 2007 meeting as the " [LINK: (Saban Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama%E2%80%93LSU_football_rivalry] ". [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-76]  [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-77]  [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-78]  The Crimson Tide lost the first "Saban Bowl" in 2007, won the 2008 and 2009 meetings only to lose in Baton Rouge in 2010.

In 2011, the teams played as the consensus No. 1 and No. 2 ranked teams in the polls with LSU winning 9–6 in overtime. They played each other again for the BCS National Championship with Alabama winning 21–0 to secure its 14th National Championship. Alabama won the most recent meeting 20–9 in Tuscaloosa and leads the series 58–27–5. [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-79]

Alabama also maintains a rivalry with the  [LINK: (Ole Miss Rebels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ole_Miss_Rebels_football] . Alabama won the most recent meeting 24–10 in Tuscaloosa, and leads the series 55–10–2. [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-80]

Alabama's most-played rival is  [LINK: (Mississippi State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mississippi_State_Bulldogs_football] . The rivalry has been called the " [LINK: (Battle for Highway 82) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama%E2%80%93Mississippi_State_football_rivalry] ", with the schools being only 90 miles apart. Many cite the 2014 meeting as the biggest game in the series where Alabama faced a #1 ranked, 9–0 Mississippi State team with  [LINK: (Dak Prescott) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dak_Prescott]  as its quarterback. Alabama won 25–20, which helped catapult them into the first College Football Playoff. Alabama won the most recent meeting 40–17 in Starkville and leads the series 86–18–3. [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-81]

The series dates back to 1900 but the rivalry has intensified in recent years, with the last four meetings having national championship implications. [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-h875-82]  Alabama leads the series 14–5. [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-i247-83]

Alabama has a rivalry with the  [LINK: (Florida Gators) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florida_Gators_football] , which was largely developed with the start of the  [LINK: (SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEC_Championship_Game] . [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-84]  Alabama and Florida have met in 10 SEC Title Games (Alabama leads 6–4 in Title games), including the first 3 from 1992 to 1994.  Alabama won the most recent meeting 31–29 in Gainesville, and leads the entire series 27–14. [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-series-85]

Alabama has a rivalry with the  [LINK: (Georgia Bulldogs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Bulldogs_football] . Georgia won the most recent meeting 28-7 in the  [LINK: (2025 SEC Championship Game) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_SEC_Championship_Game]  while Alabama leads the series 45–27-4. [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-86]

The  [LINK: (Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama-Georgia_Tech_football_rivalry]  were at one time considered Alabama's arch rival.[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ] During the suspension of the Iron Bowl between 1907 and 1948, Georgia Tech (then a member of the SEC) emerged as the most intense game on Alabama's schedule. The teams played many significant games, especially in the late 1950s and early 1960s. A heated feud developed between Bear Bryant and Georgia Tech head coach  [LINK: (Bobby Dodd) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobby_Dodd]  following a controversial hit in the  [LINK: (1961) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1961_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team]  game, a 10–0 Alabama victory. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-87]  Dodd cited this feud as the primary impetus for Georgia Tech leaving the SEC three years later. [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-88]  The two teams have met 52 times, making Georgia Tech Alabama's most played among current non-conference opponents. Alabama leads the series 28–21–3; Georgia Tech won the last meeting in  [LINK: (1984) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1984_Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football_team] . Alabama's fight song, "Yea Alabama", mentions Georgia Tech with the line "Send the Yellow Jackets to a watery grave." [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama_Crimson_Tide_football#cite_note-89]

There have been many historic games between  [LINK: (Alabama and Penn State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabama%E2%80%93Penn_State_football_rivalry] . The two teams met five times during the tenure of Bear Bryant, including in the  [LINK: (1979 Sugar Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1979_Sugar_Bowl] , which determined the national championship for the 1978 season. The games usually have national implications—seven of the 15 meetings between the two schools have featured both teams ranked in the top ten—and eight of the meetings have been decided by a touchdown or less. The most recent game was in 2011, with Alabama winning 27–11. It was the final loss for long-time Penn State head coach  [LINK: (Joe Paterno) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Paterno] . Alabama leads the series 10–5.


